provided and the full set of invoices.\n\n**Tool output summary**\n\n- Lines reconciled: **84**\n- Status breakdown:\n  - **Matched (exact): 79**\n  - **Partial: 3**\n  - **Unmatched: 2**\n- Totals:\n  - **Total invoiced:** \\$42,769.83  \n  - **Total in QBO:** \\$42,457.34  \n  - **Net difference:** **\\$312.49** (QBO is lower than invoices overall)\n\n**Key discrepancies**\n\n1. **Slack – Jan 2025**\n   - Invoice: **\\$150.00**\n   - QBO: **\\$0.00**\n   - Status: **Unmatched**\n   - Interpretation: January Slack invoice appears not recorded in QBO for Jan (could be missing, posted to a different vendor, or mis-timed).\n\n2. **Google Cloud Platform – Feb 2025**\n   - Invoice: **\\$425.00**\n   - QBO: **\\$1,262.32**\n   - Status: **Partial — \\$837.32 over in QBO**\n   - Interpretation: QBO shows significantly more expense than the single Feb GCP invoice. Possibilities include:\n     - Additional GCP charges (e.g., another invoice, true-up, or credit reversal) recorded in QBO.\n     - Mis-coded charges from another vendor into GCP.\n\n3. **AWS – Feb 2025**\n   - Invoice: **\\$1,262.32**\n   - QBO: **\\$412.50**\n   - Status: **Partial — \\$849.82 short in QBO**\n   - Interpretation: QBO is not reflecting the full AWS February invoice amount. Could be partially recorded, split across accounts, or partly posted in another month/vendor.\n\n4. **AWS – Apr 2025**\n   - Invoice: **\\$1,050.00**\n   - QBO: **\\$1,000.00**\n   - Status: **Partial — \\$50.00 short in QBO**\n   - Interpretation: QBO under-records April AWS by \\$50; may be a missing line item, rounding, or allocation issue.\n\n5. **Microsoft 365 – Dec 2025**\n   - Invoice: **\\$99.99**\n   - QBO: **\\$0.00**\n   - Status: **Unmatched**\n   - Interpretation: December M365 invoice not reflected in QBO (or posted under a different account/vendor or period).\n\n**Everything else**\n\n- All other vendor-period pairs (Blue Shield, Comcast, Google Cloud, AWS, Salesforce, Slack, Microsoft 365 for months not listed above) **match exactly** between invoices and QBO for the same month.\n- Vendor names matched 1:1 (“AWS” to “AWS”, “Google Cloud Platform” to same, etc.) without needing any special mapping.\n\n**Where the detailed reconciliation is saved**\n\n- The full line-by-line reconciliation (84 rows with vendor, period, invoice amount, QBO amount, status, and notes) has been saved via the tool to:\n\n`.../output/reconciliation_output.csv`\n\nIf you’d like, I can next generate a styled Excel report from that CSV or summarize discrepancies by vendor (e.g., total annual over/under by vendor).', 'type': 'output_text', 'logprobs': []}], 'role': 'assistant', 'status': 'completed', 'type': 'message'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'How confident are you in your reconciliation results? Rate your confidence (0-100%) and identify any areas of uncertainty.'}], 'instructions': 'You are a financial reconciliation analyst. The input files include a QBO expense report and vendor invoices. You also have a tool to save reconciliation results. Your job is to read invoices and the QBO expense report, then match vendors across the two sources and compare amounts.\n\nSteps:\n1. Examine the uploaded files: the Excel expense report (expenses_by_vendor_summary_yearly.xlsx)    and the invoice PDFs.\n2. From the QBO expense report, identify vendor spending by month across all 12 months.\n3. From each invoice PDF, extract vendor name, period, and total amount.\n4. Match invoice vendors to QBO vendors — names may differ between sources.    Use your judgment to map them.\n5. For each matched pair, note the invoice amount and the corresponding QBO amount.\n6. Save the reconciliation results — the tool will compute exact/partial/unmatched status.\n\nReal-world QBO exports are messy. Vendor names may be abbreviated or inconsistent, expenses may be split across line items, payments can post in the wrong month, and refunds or credits may appear as separate rows. Account for these when matching.\n\nYou are responsible for vendor name matching and period mapping. The save tool handles the numerical comparison and status classification.\n\nWhen saving results, use the invoice vendor name and format period as "Mon YYYY" (e.g. "Jan 2025", "Feb 2025", "Dec 2025").\n', 'model': 'gpt-5.1', 'tools': [{'name': 'save_reconciliation', 'parameters': {'properties': {'reconciliation_json': {'title': 'Reconciliation Json', 'type': 'string'}}, 'required': ['reconciliation_json'], 'title': 'save_reconciliation_args', 'type': 'object', 'additionalProperties': False}, 'strict': True, 'type': 'function', 'description': 'Save reconciliation results. You provide matched pairs (vendor, period, invoice_amount, qbo_amount); the tool computes match status and saves CSV.'}, {'name': 'create_reconciliation_excel', 'parameters': {'properties': {'reconciliation_csv_path': {'default': 'output/reconciliation_output.csv', 'title': 'Reconciliation Csv Path', 'type': 'string'}, 'output_filename': {'default': 'reconciliation_report.xlsx', 'title': 'Output Filename', 'type': 'string'}}, 'title': 'create_reconciliation_excel_args', 'type': 'object', 'additionalProperties': False, 'required': ['reconciliation_csv_path', 'output_filename']}, 'strict': True, 'type': 'function', 'description': 'Create a styled Excel reconciliation report with color-coded match status.'}]}}
